1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,337
In my last video, I showed you a software factory, built on Claude Code.

2
00:00:04,337 --> 00:00:06,766
This time, I want to tackle its weakest link.

3
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:09,033
Be honest.

4
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:12,941
How many times have you handed an AI a Figma file, or a screenshot…

5
00:00:12,941 --> 00:00:13,991
and it said, done?

6
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,032
And then you actually looked at it.

7
00:00:16,032 --> 00:00:17,373
The names are wrapping.

8
00:00:17,373 --> 00:00:18,831
The table's way too tall.

9
00:00:18,831 --> 00:00:20,173
Nothing quite lines up.

10
00:00:20,173 --> 00:00:21,514
And this isn't made up.

11
00:00:21,514 --> 00:00:23,905
It's a real attempt, from my own factory.

12
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:30,115
So how do you prove a screen looks right, without a person checking every pixel?

13
00:00:30,115 --> 00:00:34,723
In this video, I'll show you the design, how I proved the checker itself works,

14
00:00:34,723 --> 00:00:37,638
a real request that failed twice before it passed…

15
00:00:37,638 --> 00:00:40,088
and how the same idea works for APIs, too.

16
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:44,019
In a software factory, verification is everything.

17
00:00:44,019 --> 00:00:46,691
And for user interfaces, it's been the weakest link.

18
00:00:46,691 --> 00:00:46,845
So…

19
00:00:46,845 --> 00:00:49,363
let's fix that, and then prove it actually works.

20
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:52,198
Here's the honest starting point.

21
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:56,596
The factory could already prove a screen had the right text and the right test ids,

22
00:00:56,596 --> 00:00:57,973
and that the tests passed.

23
00:00:57,973 --> 00:01:01,470
But it couldn't tell you whether the screen actually looked right.

24
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:02,795
So, a person had to look.

25
00:01:02,795 --> 00:01:04,490
And wherever verification stops…

26
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:05,497
autonomy stops too.

27
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:09,015
So the idea is three layers.

28
00:01:09,015 --> 00:01:09,910
First, measured.

29
00:01:09,910 --> 00:01:14,103
A script checks what can be measured, and gives the same answer every time.

30
00:01:14,103 --> 00:01:14,942
Second, judged.

31
00:01:14,942 --> 00:01:19,470
A separate agent looks at the images, and has to cite evidence for every finding.

32
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:20,588
And third, accepted.

33
00:01:20,588 --> 00:01:24,726
A person sees the reference, the result, and the difference, side by side.

34
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:28,375
Here's how it fits into the pipeline.

35
00:01:28,375 --> 00:01:31,341
A screenshot attached to the issue becomes the reference.

36
00:01:31,341 --> 00:01:34,931
The visual spec turns it into a contract, and a person approves that,

37
00:01:34,931 --> 00:01:36,232
before anything is built.

38
00:01:36,232 --> 00:01:38,209
The plan gives the page a visual gate.

39
00:01:38,209 --> 00:01:39,926
The checker measures, and judges.

40
00:01:39,926 --> 00:01:42,060
After the tests, the verifier runs again.

41
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:45,806
And when the person approves the result, those screens become baselines.

42
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:49,641
The measured layer is one small script.

43
00:01:49,641 --> 00:01:54,080
It renders the screen in headless Chrome, then for each region in the contract,

44
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:55,316
it checks five things.

45
00:01:55,316 --> 00:01:55,990
Is it there.

46
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:57,395
Is it in the right place.

47
00:01:57,395 --> 00:01:58,968
Does it say the right words.

48
00:01:58,968 --> 00:02:00,260
Is it the right colour.

49
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:01,665
And does it look similar.

50
00:02:01,665 --> 00:02:03,462
Every number goes into a report.

51
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:07,697
Now, here's the part most teams skip.

52
00:02:07,697 --> 00:02:09,457
A verifier nobody has tested…

53
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:10,611
is just an opinion.

54
00:02:10,611 --> 00:02:13,586
So I tested it the way you'd test any other code.

55
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:20,089
I took a screen the factory had already built, and planted one defect at a time.

56
00:02:20,089 --> 00:02:20,901
Like this one.

57
00:02:20,901 --> 00:02:22,988
The totals moved twenty-four pixels.

58
00:02:22,988 --> 00:02:24,960
The region goes red on both sides,

59
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:27,859
and the difference panel shows exactly what moved.

60
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:30,648
A swapped accent colour.

61
00:02:30,648 --> 00:02:31,997
Caught by the colour check.

62
00:02:31,997 --> 00:02:34,094
The difference is measured, not eyeballed.

63
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:37,719
A table row that wraps onto two lines.

64
00:02:37,719 --> 00:02:38,973
The box barely moved…

65
00:02:38,973 --> 00:02:40,466
but similarity caught it.

66
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:45,101
Six planted defects, and all six were caught.

67
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:47,810
And just as important, three harmless changes,

68
00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:50,638
like different sample data or a sub-pixel shift…

69
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:51,933
and zero false alarms.

70
00:02:51,933 --> 00:02:56,233
It runs in presubmit, so if anyone weakens the verifier, the build fails.

71
00:02:57,450 --> 00:02:58,604
And it's deterministic.

72
00:02:58,604 --> 00:03:02,168
The same build, verified twice, gives a byte-for-byte identical report.

73
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:03,776
Okay.

74
00:03:03,776 --> 00:03:06,448
Now, let's run a real request through it…

75
00:03:06,448 --> 00:03:07,165
end to end.

76
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:12,812
The client attaches a screenshot to the issue, and then writes what they want,

77
00:03:12,812 --> 00:03:13,651
in plain words.

78
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:18,113
The intake saves that screenshot with the spec,

79
00:03:18,113 --> 00:03:21,060
and the visual spec agent writes a contract from it.

80
00:03:22,450 --> 00:03:24,462
And here's something I didn't plan for.

81
00:03:24,462 --> 00:03:26,784
The first contract the agent wrote was wrong.

82
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:29,932
It measured the heading's text instead of the heading itself,

83
00:03:29,932 --> 00:03:33,080
and it demanded a total that only existed in the sample data.

84
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,382
Approve that, and a correct build would have failed three times.

85
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:39,136
So I didn't patch the contract.

86
00:03:39,136 --> 00:03:41,311
I fixed the skill that writes contracts.

87
00:03:41,311 --> 00:03:42,943
That's the meta loop, working.

88
00:03:42,943 --> 00:03:44,955
And the second contract got it right.

89
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:49,271
The client approves the contract, along with the requirements,

90
00:03:49,271 --> 00:03:50,908
before a single line of code exists.

91
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:54,912
The plan gives the page section a fourth gate.

92
00:03:54,912 --> 00:03:56,945
A visual check, against the reference.

93
00:03:58,450 --> 00:04:00,298
And this is where it gets interesting.

94
00:04:00,298 --> 00:04:03,800
The checker failed the page twice, for two completely different reasons.

95
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:07,842
The first time, the measured layer caught it.

96
00:04:07,842 --> 00:04:12,043
Names were wrapping, and the table was four hundred and sixty five pixels tall,

97
00:04:12,043 --> 00:04:14,701
against three hundred and twenty in the reference.

98
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:17,300
The second time…

99
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:18,842
the verifier actually passed.

100
00:04:18,842 --> 00:04:20,330
The maker had overcorrected.

101
00:04:20,330 --> 00:04:21,606
Tiny text, cramped rows.

102
00:04:21,606 --> 00:04:25,061
The numbers squeaked through, but the checker's judgement didn't.

103
00:04:25,061 --> 00:04:27,187
That's exactly why there are two layers.

104
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:30,005
Look at them side by side.

105
00:04:30,005 --> 00:04:31,142
Every box is green…

106
00:04:31,142 --> 00:04:33,954
but the table has shrunk, and the text is tiny.

107
00:04:33,954 --> 00:04:36,586
A script on its own would have shipped this.

108
00:04:38,450 --> 00:04:40,023
On the third try, it passed.

109
00:04:40,023 --> 00:04:42,326
Then the outer loop ran everything again.

110
00:04:42,326 --> 00:04:45,360
The evidence, the tests, and the verifier, on its own.

111
00:04:46,450 --> 00:04:48,298
Reference, built, and difference.

112
00:04:48,298 --> 00:04:49,361
Every region green.

113
00:04:49,361 --> 00:04:51,881
The only differences left are font rendering,

114
00:04:51,881 --> 00:04:54,512
because the runner draws text with Linux fonts.

115
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:58,772
And that image goes straight into the gate notice.

116
00:04:58,772 --> 00:05:01,280
The person approving doesn't have to dig for anything.

117
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:04,048
Here's the dashboard, running.

118
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:08,965
And when it's approved, those screens become baselines.

119
00:05:08,965 --> 00:05:10,382
The merge request carries them,

120
00:05:10,382 --> 00:05:13,583
so the next change to this screen gets checked against them, strictly.

121
00:05:15,450 --> 00:05:17,597
Running it for real taught me three things.

122
00:05:17,597 --> 00:05:19,744
A git quirk silently dropped the reference.

123
00:05:19,744 --> 00:05:22,091
The factory's own guardrail is what exposed it.

124
00:05:22,091 --> 00:05:25,986
An agent's contract can be wrong, and that's exactly why a person approves it.

125
00:05:25,986 --> 00:05:29,382
And a screenshot from one machine never matches pixels from another.

126
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:32,522
And this isn't only about screens.

127
00:05:32,522 --> 00:05:34,412
The pattern is always the same.

128
00:05:34,412 --> 00:05:35,692
Agree on a reference.

129
00:05:35,692 --> 00:05:36,972
Measure what you can.

130
00:05:36,972 --> 00:05:38,252
Judge what you can't.

131
00:05:38,252 --> 00:05:41,178
And keep what a person accepts, as the baseline.

132
00:05:41,178 --> 00:05:43,494
For an API, the reference is the spec.

133
00:05:43,494 --> 00:05:46,481
Status codes, schemas and contracts get measured,

134
00:05:46,481 --> 00:05:51,541
a reviewer agent judges the behaviour, and recorded responses become the baselines.

135
00:05:51,541 --> 00:05:54,710
And it works the same way for data, and for reports.

136
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:57,297
Now, the limits.

137
00:05:57,297 --> 00:06:01,320
Boxes estimated from an image are approximate, so the tolerance is generous.

138
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:05,713
The judged layer is still a model's opinion, it's just forced to show its evidence.

139
00:06:05,713 --> 00:06:08,413
And so far, it's been proven on one kind of screen.

140
00:06:10,450 --> 00:06:14,638
Verification is what turns agents writing code into a factory you can trust.

141
00:06:14,638 --> 00:06:15,961
The code is open source,

142
00:06:15,961 --> 00:06:19,267
and there's a test drive account if you want to look around.

143
00:06:19,267 --> 00:06:20,370
Thanks for watching.
